abstract: >-
  Minimax is a value, not a policy. At 44.7% of teacher decisions in
  tic-tac-toe the optimal move is not unique, so a deterministic teacher
  collapses each optimal equivalence class to one action. Nine recurrent
  checkpoints that draw 100% of games against a fixed depth-6 teacher draw a
  median of 24.3% against equally strong twins that differ only in tie-breaking
  (median gap 75.8 points); an exact depth-9 control reproduces the gap. An
  exact backward-induction certificate over every optimal teacher gives each
  checkpoint a worst case W and best case B: all nine fixed checkpoints have
  W = 0 and B = 1. The certificate is then turned into a method,
  certificate-guided repair (CGR): its violating frontier histories are added,
  with exact targets, to an aggregate that is refitted and re-certified until
  W = 1. Optimality on the frontier is proved sufficient for W = 1 and each
  successful round adds a new state, so the loop terminates with an exact
  guarantee. Seven certificate-selected histories take C-42 from W = 0 to
  W = 1; five repair a GRU trained on all 180,361 histories; three repair a
  policy that loses to every optimal teacher. Matched random aggregation with
  the same number of states, even from the 5,097-state optimal-play tree, never
  certifies. The paper also retracts several v1 claims after audit.
